WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.250
Whether the point of it is to reward fans,
finally push past Avatar in ticket sales,

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.460
act as an advertisement for the home release,
or all of the above, the Avengers: Endgame

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.600
re-release is here with new footage.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.740
Here's a closer look at the bonus materials.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.200
After the credits, have some tissues ready
for a tribute to one of the builders of the

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.540
Marvel Universe: Stan "The Man" Lee.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.800
Most of the footage comes from the sets of
the various MCU films in which Lee had cameos.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.890
There are snippets of interviews, including
one Lee gives while dressed like Hugh Hefner

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.279
for his cameo in 2008's Iron Man.

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:36.809
James Gunn proudly declares Stan's greatness
as Lee prepares to shoot his cameo in 2017's

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:38.680
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.460
"At that time, I was a Federal Express man…"

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.360
The casts and crews are all charmed by the
charismatic old writer and editor.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:50.820
Chris Evans belts out big laughs as the two
sit at nearby tables in an outdoor cafe in

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.080
a deleted scene from 2012's Avengers.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.539
Lee forces Chris Hemsworth to break character
after the actor delivers his line in Age of

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.139
Ultron, which was apparently Lee's favorite
cameo.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.940
"Stop tryin' to scare us.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:02.800
C'mon."

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.280
Brie Larson hugs him so sweetly on the set
of 2018's Captain Marvel you'd think he was

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.340
her own grandpa.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.750
At the end of the tribute, the words "Stan,
We Love You 3000" appear, setting off the

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.200
waterworks for sure.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.440
The deleted scene included with the re-release
opens on what may remind you of the 1988 action

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.560
classic Die Hard.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.420
"Welcome to the party, pal!"

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.140
An office building is on fire, and below,
emergency crews struggle to save the few people

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.040
left inside.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:36.000
Actor Reginald VelJohnson who plays LAPD Sergeant
Al Powell in Die Hard calls urgently for "more

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:36.500
choppers."

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.480
In the sky we see something leap from one
building to another, crushing walls as it

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:40.980
goes.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.840
Eventually, the Hulk bursts from the building
and lands on the ground near the emergency

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.619
workers, with the endangered citizens in the
bowl of a satellite dish.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:52.369
Having saved the day, the Hulk gives a gracious
wave you'd expect from Superman.

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:55.119
He then gets a call, presumably from Captain
America.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.920
After picking up, Hulk walks offscreen, saying
"Steve who?"

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.780
The scene is unfinished, so the Hulk we get
is a pre-visualized, less physically imposing

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.090
character than would have appeared in the
final film.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.920
From the context, it seems the scene was meant
to come between the Avengers' first failed

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.281
attempt to get Tony Stark to help them figure
out time travel and the hilarious scene in

00:02:13.281 --> 00:02:15.680
the diner when they all meet with Professor
Hulk.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.720
In the post-Snap Marvel Cinematic Universe,
the Hulk seems to be the only Avenger acting

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:21.560
like an Avenger.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.750
All we see Cap do is attend a support group
and hang out with Black Widow at the Avengers

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:26.240
compound.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.740
We get the impression Widow doesn't leave
the compound all that much.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.450
Clint Barton has gone rogue and is slaughtering
gangsters across the globe.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.080
It's only the often-rampaging Hulk we see
acting like a superhero.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.450
And why is Bruce Banner free and not on the
run?

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.060
Before his return to Earth in 2018's Avengers:
Infinity War, the Hulk was wanted for his

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.030
rampage in 2015's Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.320
Now that he's back, has all been forgiven?

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.220
Did he work something out with the government?

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.290
Or is he technically still wanted, but no
one wants to be the one to try to slap cuffs

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:55.780
on him?

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.480
Are there simply bigger fish to fry?

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.940
After the tribute and the deleted scene, there's
something much more like the post-credits

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.820
and mid-credits scenes we're used to.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.030
In this case, there seems to be part of a
scene pulled directly from the upcoming Spider-Man:

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:08.780
Far from Home.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.020
Anyone who's been paying attention to the
trailers and TV spots for the new Spidey flick

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.780
will likely recognize many shots and lines
of dialogue.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.480
Nick Fury and Maria Hill drive to the devastated
town of Ixtenco, Mexico.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.100
We don't know exactly what happened, but Fury
mentions a cyclone that locals said "had a

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:25.600
face."

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.080
Mysterio appears in a column of green smoke
and is confused when he sees the S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.280
operatives pointing their guns at him.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.430
One of the destructive Elementals appears,
and Fury and Hill turn to face it.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.700
Mysterio warns:

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.720
"You don't want any part of this."

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.290
Then Mysterio blasts green energy at the creature,
and the scene cuts to the Spider-Man: Far

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.280
from Home logo.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.900
One of the more interesting aspects of the
Far from Home preview is that it's set in

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:52.400
Mexico.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.320
It seems strange that Fury and Hill's first
encounter with both Mysterio and one of the

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.370
elementals would be set in North America while
presumably the rest of the action will be

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:01.040
in Europe.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.920
There's also the question of exactly why Fury
and Hill are in Mexico.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.000
When they leave the car, Hill seems annoyed.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.820
She tells Fury that the devastation we see
in Ixtenco is tragic, but, quote, "it's not

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:12.820
why we're here."

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.150
So why are they there?

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.120
What are they investigating?

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.440
Will it turn out that Hill is wrong, and that
Ixtenco's destruction directly relates to

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:19.940
their mission?

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.960
Or is it completely unrelated?

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.340
One of the great things about the MCU is that
when it comes to questions like this, there's

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.540
room to be surprised.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.560
In any other type of film, one could assume
that Fury and Hill's unnamed mission will

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.160
be related to that film's story, otherwise
it wouldn't even be mentioned.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.310
But in a Marvel movie?

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.020
You never know.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.680
With the long game they play and how they
love to tease us, Fury and Hill's Mexico mission

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.471
could be about Far from Home, or could also
be related to Eternals, Black Panther 2, or

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.110
another future film we haven't even heard
about yet.

